{"topic": "science", "script": "For most of human history, work stopped when the sun went down. Artificial light changed that, but it didn't change how our bodies work. Every one of us carries an internal clock, run by a small cluster of cells in the brain, that expects darkness to mean rest. When we override it, with a bright screen at midnight or a shift that starts at two in the morning, the clock doesn't simply adjust. It resists. Studies of night-shift workers have found higher rates of digestive problems and disrupted mood, even years into the job, long after they'd supposedly gotten used to the schedule. The strange part is that the body never seems to fully switch over. A night-shift nurse who sleeps every day from eight in the morning until four in the afternoon is still, according to her internal clock, sleeping at the wrong time, because that clock keeps taking its cues from daylight, whether or not she's awake to see it. Some hospitals have started dimming lights gradually near the end of a night shift, hoping to ease the transition. It's a small fix for a mismatch that modern life created and hasn't solved.", "questions": [{"kind": "main_idea", "prompt": "The recording is mainly concerned with", "correct_answer": "why the body's internal clock resists working against natural daylight, even after years of adaptation", "distractors": ["a comparison of sleep quality between different professions", "the discovery of the brain cells that control the internal clock", "advice for hospitals on how to schedule night shifts"], "explanation": "The recording opens with the internal clock's resistance to artificial schedules and closes with hospitals only partly easing that mismatch. No discovery is described, and profession comparison and scheduling advice are not its subject.", "difficulty_est": 3}, {"kind": "detail", "prompt": "According to the recording, the internal clock is run by", "correct_answer": "a small cluster of cells in the brain", "distractors": ["th